##### MF1: GENERAL INFORMATION                                    1325 1451  233
                                                                  1325 1451  234
- MT451    : Descriptive data and directory                       1325 1451  235
                                                                  1325 1451  236
This text and the full directory of used MF/MT sections.          1325 1451  237
                                                                  1325 1451  238
##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   1325 1451  239
                                                                  1325 1451  240
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 1325 1451  241
                                                                  1325 1451  242
Details about the origin of the resonance parameters for this     1325 1451  243
nuclide are given above in this description. In general, both a   1325 1451  244
Resolved and an Unresolved Resonance Range are given.             1325 1451  245
                                                                  1325 1451  246
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                1325 1451  247
                                                                  1325 1451  248
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       1325 1451  249
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  1325 1451  250
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   1325 1451  251
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  1325 1451  252
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  1325 1451  253
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 1325 1451  254
to the highest incident energy.                                   1325 1451  255
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:1325 1451  256
                                                                  1325 1451  257
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  1325 1451  258
                                                                  1325 1451  259
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    1325 1451  260
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     1325 1451  261
                                                                  1325 1451  262
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     1325 1451  263
                                                                  1325 1451  264
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    1325 1451  265
total cross section.                                              1325 1451  266
                                                                  1325 1451  267
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            1325 1451  268
                                                                  1325 1451  269
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    1325 1451  270
                                                                  1325 1451  271
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        1325 1451  272
                                                                  1325 1451  273
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  1325 1451  274
                                                                  1325 1451  275
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           1325 1451  276
                                                                  1325 1451  277
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   1325 1451  278
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        1325 1451  279
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     1325 1451  280
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         1325 1451  281
sections.                                                         1325 1451  282
                                                                  1325 1451  283
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 1325 1451  284
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 1325 1451  285
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 1325 1451  286
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 1325 1451  287
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      1325 1451  288
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       1325 1451  289
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              1325 1451  290
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  1325 1451  291
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  1325 1451  292
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  1325 1451  293
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  1325 1451  294
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  1325 1451  295
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1325 1451  296
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        1325 1451  297
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1325 1451  298
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        1325 1451  299
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1325 1451  300
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        1325 1451  301
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1325 1451  302
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        1325 1451  303
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1325 1451  304
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        1325 1451  305
                                                                  1325 1451  306
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           1325 1451  307
                                                                  1325 1451  308
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    1325 1451  309
discrete levels.                                                  1325 1451  310
                                                                  1325 1451  311
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         1325 1451  312
                                                                  1325 1451  313
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  1325 1451  314
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   1325 1451  315
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      1325 1451  316
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  1325 1451  317
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      1325 1451  318
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         1325 1451  319
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   1325 1451  320
on an angular grid.                                               1325 1451  321
                                                                  1325 1451  322
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       1325 1451  323
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1325 1451  324
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1325 1451  325
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1325 1451  326
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1325 1451  327
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1325 1451  328
                                                                  1325 1451  329
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     1325 1451  330
                                                                  1325 1451  331
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     1325 1451  332
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   1325 1451  333
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  1325 1451  334
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             1325 1451  335
All data are generated with TALYS.                                1325 1451  336
                                                                  1325 1451  337
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   1325 1451  338
                                                                  1325 1451  339
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      1325 1451  340
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   1325 1451  341
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   1325 1451  342
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are1325 1451  343
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   1325 1451  344
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  1325 1451  345
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              1325 1451  346
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 1325 1451  347
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  1325 1451  348
For each residual product:                                        1325 1451  349
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               1325 1451  350
- the recoil                                                      1325 1451  351
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         1325 1451  352
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     1325 1451  353
sections.                                                         1325 1451  354
                                                                  1325 1451  355
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     1325 1451  356
                                                                  1325 1451  357
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  1325 1451  358
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 1325 1451  359
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          1325 1451  360
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       1325 1451  361
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 1325 1451  362
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     1325 1451  363
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  1325 1451  364
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      1325 1451  365
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         1325 1451  366
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             1325 1451  367
                                                                  1325 1451  368
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   1325 1451  369
followed:                                                         1325 1451  370
                                                                  1325 1451  371
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1325 1451  372
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1325 1451  373
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1325 1451  374
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     1325 1451  375
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1325 1451  376
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1325 1451  377
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1325 1451  378
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1325 1451  379
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1325 1451  380
                                                                  1325 1451  381
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 1325 1451  382
                                                                  1325 1451  383
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    1325 1451  384
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          1325 1451  385
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     1325 1451  386
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          1325 1451  387
                                                                  1325 1451  388
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  1325 1451  389
                                                                  1325 1451  390
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     1325 1451  391
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1325 1451  392
as isomer.                                                        1325 1451  393
                                                                  1325 1451  394
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 1325 1451  395
                                                                  1325 1451  396
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        1325 1451  397
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1325 1451  398
as isomer.                                                        1325 1451  399
                                                                  1325 1451  400
##### MF12: PHOTON PRODUCTION YIELD DATA                          1325 1451  401
                                                                  1325 1451  402
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:1325 1451  403
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   1325 1451  404
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   1325 1451  405
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   1325 1451  406
  given                                                           1325 1451  407
                                                                  1325 1451  408
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          1325 1451  409
                                                                  1325 1451  410
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  1325 1451  411
                                                                  1325 1451  412
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 1325 1451  413
                                                                  1325 1451  414
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         1325 1451  415
                                                                  1325 1451  416
##### MF32: COVARIANCES OF RESONANCE PARAMETERS                   1325 1451  417
                                                                  1325 1451  418
In MF32, we store the covariances of resonance parameters,        1325 1451  419
and more details are given above in this description. In most     1325 1451  420
versions of TENDL, this information is now stored in MF33.        1325 1451  421
                                                                  1325 1451  422
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 1325 1451  423
                                                                  1325 1451  424
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  1325 1451  425
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     1325 1451  426
with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates TALYS runs using    1325 1451  427
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   1325 1451  428
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  1325 1451  429
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                1325 1451  430
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      1325 1451  431
   same reaction channel between different energies)              1325 1451  432
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      1325 1451  433
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               1325 1451  434
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      1325 1451  435
   different reaction channels for different energies)            1325 1451  436
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        1325 1451  437
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              1325 1451  438
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  1325 1451  439
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       1325 1451  440
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     1325 1451  441
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  1325 1451  442
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     1325 1451  443
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     1325 1451  444
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     1325 1451  445
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   1325 1451  446
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   1325 1451  447
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  1325 1451  448
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   1325 1451  449
                                                                  1325 1451  450
##### MF34: COVARIANCES OF ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                  1325 1451  451
                                                                  1325 1451  452
In MF34, we store the covariances of the elastic scattering       1325 1451  453
angular distributions that are given in MF4/MT2. All covariances  1325 1451  454
are generated with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates       1325 1451  455
TALYS runs using a Monte Carlo parameter method. The tables are   1325 1451  456
restricted to MT2 and contain covariances for the first 6         1325 1451  457
Legendre coefficients. Both intra- and inter-correlations of the  1325 1451  458
various Legendre coefficients are given. As in MF33, the LB5      1325 1451  459
representation is used.                                           1325 1451  460
                                                                  1325 1451  461
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      1325 1451  462
                                                                  1325 1451  463
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  1325 1451  464
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             1325 1451  465
                                                                  1325 1451  466
